WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
INTRO:
We all know Apple as the world’s largest company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:09.520
the world’s most profitable company, and arguably&nbsp;
the world’s most revolutionary company. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.240
did you know that Apple is also one of the most&nbsp;
indebted companies? As of June 2022, Apple had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.400
$10.9 billion in outstanding commercial paper,&nbsp;
$14 billion worth of short term debt, and $48.8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.880
billion in other current liabilities. They also&nbsp;
had a total of $94.7 billion in long term debt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.000
$53.6 billion in other non-current liabilities.&nbsp;
All of this adds up to a total of $222 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.120
worth of debt. Given Apple’s insane profitability&nbsp;
along with their massive cash reserves, you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.360
think that Apple would pay this debt of ASAP, but&nbsp;
they’ve actually been doing the exact opposite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.840
Over the past several years, Apple has&nbsp;
been consistently taking on new debt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.400
If you take a look at their total liabilities over&nbsp;
time, you’ll see that they’ve 10Xed their debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.560
over the past decade. And even just a few weeks&nbsp;
ago, they raised another $5.5 billion. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.480
same time, Apple has also been vigorously reducing&nbsp;
their cash reserves. Since the beginning of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.800
Apple has reduced their cash reserves from&nbsp;
$107 billion to just under $50 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.520
So, while Apple is indeed more profitable than&nbsp;
ever before, they’re also more leveraged than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.080
ever before with a debt to equity ratio of over 2.&nbsp;
So, why in the world does Apple have so much debt?

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.600
ECONOMIC REASONING:&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.560
Starting off with the reasoning that applies to&nbsp;
not just Apple but all of us, we have the economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.480
reasoning. By this point, I’m sure all of you guys&nbsp;
are well aware of how high inflation is. In July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.440
we saw inflation reading peak above 9%, and&nbsp;
in the most recent inflation reading, we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.120
inflation come in at 8.6%. People are hopeful that&nbsp;
this decline means that the peak for inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.840
is already in, but even if that’s true, we’re&nbsp;
likely going to see elevated inflation readings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.160
for atleast another year. So, it’s smart to&nbsp;
leverage strategies that benefit from high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.160
inflation and one of those strategies is taking&nbsp;
on more debt. Now, at first, this may seem a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.280
counterintuitive. When inflation is high, the cost&nbsp;
of production skyrockets which makes it difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.520
for companies to maintain their margins. So,&nbsp;
psychologically, the last thing that you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.120
want to do is add on more debt and take on yet&nbsp;
another regular payment. But, in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.320
this is actually the best time to be taking&nbsp;
out debt. You see, if you lock in a loan today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.920
your payments themselves will be the same for the&nbsp;
entire duration of the loan. However, this cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.440
be said about the value of payments. With high&nbsp;
inflation, the real value of each payment will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.240
be consistently declining. For example, paying&nbsp;
$1000 mortgage in 1990 is not the same as paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.840
a $1000 mortgage in 2022. The 2022 mortgage is&nbsp;
far cheaper despite being the same dollar value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.160
Companies are able to take this idea to&nbsp;
even the next level because their debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.400
generally works differently from the debt that&nbsp;
we’re used to. For example, with a mortgage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.280
you’ll be paying principle and interest every&nbsp;
single month. Interest makes up a larger portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.680
in the beginning and a smaller portion near the&nbsp;
end, but regardless, you’re paying interest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.720
principal with every payment. With corporate debt,&nbsp;
however, companies generally only make interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.600
payments for the duration of the debt. And it’s&nbsp;
not till the end of the term that companies pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.880
back the principal. Now, this might not sound like&nbsp;
a big deal, but it is and here’s why. Let’s say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:31.040
Apple took out $1 worth of debt in 1970 at a 3%&nbsp;
interest rate for 30 years. Interest rates were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.880
obviously not that low in the 1970s, but that was&nbsp;
the last time we saw inflation at these levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.440
so that’s why we’re using this time period&nbsp;
Anyway, this would mean that Apple has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.320
pay 3 cents per year in interest which works&nbsp;
out to a total of 90 cents over 30 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.320
This is quite a substantial amount given that&nbsp;
the interest itself is nearly equivalent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.960
the initial loan value of $1. But, Apple would&nbsp;
actually come out ahead by quite a margin. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.280
see, Apple didn’t have to pay back the initial $1&nbsp;
till 2000, and by that point, one 2000 dollar was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:10.480
only worth 23 1970 cents. So essentially, Apple&nbsp;
borrowed a dollar and only paid back 23 cents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.160
You would of course also have to factor in the&nbsp;
cost of the interest, but Apple comes out on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.680
by a large amount. Considering this, you might be&nbsp;
asking, why in the world would anybody loan Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.880
any money when they know that Apple is gonna come&nbsp;
out ahead. Well, the answer is that the lender’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.680
alternative options are even worse. Usually, the&nbsp;
people that are lending these companies money are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.360
banks, pension funds, and conservative individuals&nbsp;
who are retiring soon. For these people, stability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.440
and liquidity are extremely important, so they&nbsp;
can’t just go out and invest in stocks. Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.320
they only have two options which are to either&nbsp;
earn 3% from Apple, Microsoft, and other large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.000
corporations or earn 0.5% from money market&nbsp;
funds and CDs or maybe 2% from treasuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.560
And from this perspective, taking the 3% deal is&nbsp;
a no brainer. So, really it’s a win win scenario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.280
Apple gets cheap money while lenders get the best&nbsp;
returns they can without going into equities.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.840
TECHNICAL REASONING:&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.080
As you can see, borrowing money at low interest&nbsp;
rates during high inflation itself is rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.560
profitable. But, Apple isn’t going around&nbsp;
and borrowing money just so they can churn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.360
interest rates. Honestly, that’s probably not&nbsp;
even worth the hassle for them. The main reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.920
they’re borrowing so much money is to actually&nbsp;
deploy it areas that produce greater returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.640
For Apple specifically, this seems to be&nbsp;
in two areas starting with stock buybacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.480
If you’re not familiar with stock buybacks,&nbsp;
this is essentially when a company goes onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.440
the stock market and buys their own shares.&nbsp;
This not only increases demand for Apple stock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.720
but it also reduces the supply of Apple stock. You&nbsp;
see, when a company buys back their own shares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.560
these shares become what is known&nbsp;
as treasury stock. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.000
companies do have the right to sell these&nbsp;
shares back on the market if they wanted to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.720
but more times than not, companies decide to&nbsp;
retire these shares which basically means to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.520
remove them from the supply permanently. Over the&nbsp;
past several years, Apple has been buying back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.800
record amounts of shares. Almost every quarter,&nbsp;
they’ve been buying back $15 to $25 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.440
worth of Apple stock which has reduced the supply&nbsp;
of Apple stock substantially. Within the last 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.160
years, the number of outstanding Apple shares&nbsp;
has fallen from 26 billion shares to 16 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.320
shares. And this is one of the main reasons that&nbsp;
Apple has been able to continue growing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.680
market cap parabolically despite already being&nbsp;
so large. Aside from funding stock buybacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.080
another reason Apple has so much debt is to&nbsp;
fund their dividends. For the longest time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.520
Apple didn’t actually pay any dividends because&nbsp;
they couldn’t afford to. But, starting in 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.920
Apple starting paying dividends, and they’ve been&nbsp;
aggressively increasing their payouts ever since.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.040
Their dividend payout has literally increased&nbsp;
10X over the past 10 years. Now, you might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:52.880
wondering, why borrow money to payout dividends?&nbsp;
Well, the first thing to establish is that Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.320
doesn’t need to borrow money to pay dividends,&nbsp;
they just choose to. And the reason they choose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.200
is to make their stock more desirable. Here’s&nbsp;
the thing, even a company like Apple can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.720
grow their revenues and profits so much. iPhone&nbsp;
sales peaked years ago and the only reason Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.560
has been able to continue growing their numbers&nbsp;
at an insane rate is thanks to their high margin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.320
services. Service related profit accounts for&nbsp;
nearly half of their gross profit even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.520
services don’t even account for a quarter of their&nbsp;
revenue. So, services have really been their cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.200
cow over the past couple of years, but sooner or&nbsp;
later that’s gonna start leveling out as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.640
And at that point, the appeal of Apple stock&nbsp;
would be lower than ever. Now, this is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.840
bad thing given that all companies eventually get&nbsp;
to this side of the S curve. But in the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.400
most companies pump up dividends to slow&nbsp;
down this process as much as possible.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.800
FUNDAMENTAL REASONING:&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:52.880
With the economic and technical reasoning out&nbsp;
of the way, we have the fundamental reasoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.000
for taking out debt. Honestly, this isn’t&nbsp;
super applicable to Apple today given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.800
they’re already past the growth phase. I mean,&nbsp;
it’s not like Apple needs to borrow money to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.480
build a new factory or develop the next big thing.&nbsp;
Now, technically, Apple does spend a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.840
amount in both of these areas, but it’s not&nbsp;
like it really grows the company all that much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.200
For example, they recently built a billion dollar&nbsp;
campus in Austin which is great and all, but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.080
not like it’s pivotal to their growth. But, just&nbsp;
because they can’t use debt to fundamentally grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.560
themselves doesn’t mean that they can’t use debt&nbsp;
to fundamentally grow the company as a whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.160
The main way they accomplish this&nbsp;
is through a bunch of acquisitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:37.120
Now, this might be a bit surprising because&nbsp;
you almost never hear about Apple acquistions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.640
on the news. But this isn’t to say that they&nbsp;
don’t do acquisitions. They actually do a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.280
of acquisitions. The reason you don’t hear about&nbsp;
them is because Apple stays away from all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.240
hot companies that you’re familiar with. Instead,&nbsp;
they buy several background companies every year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.920
usually for a few hundred million each. In 2017,&nbsp;
for example, Apple completed 12 acquisitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:04.560
In 2018, they completed 10. In 2019, the completed&nbsp;
6. And in 2020, they completed 9. And that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.680
the ones that are listed on wikipedia. According&nbsp;
to Apple Insider, Apple usually completes between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.320
15 and 20 acquisitions per year. Usually, these&nbsp;
acquisitions help them advance their current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:19.440
products or develop new products. For example,&nbsp;
in 2020, they acquired an AR company called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.320
Camerai and a VR company called Spaces. These&nbsp;
acquisitions support the rumors that Apple will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.400
be coming out with their own AR and VR products&nbsp;
which will of course help the company continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:33.360
growing fundamentally. They’re not limited to just&nbsp;
buying up startups either. They can go out and buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.920
stock in their partners or even competitors. If&nbsp;
we take a look back at Apple’s balance sheet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.440
we’ll see that they have $131 billion in long term&nbsp;
marketable securities. So, it’s very possible that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.840
a portion of this has gone towards buying the&nbsp;
stocks and bonds of other fortune 500 companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:53.920
Technically, Apple doesn’t need to report a stake&nbsp;
in a public company unless its larger than 5%. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.920
Apple could have a $100 billion stake in&nbsp;
Microsoft or a $50 billion stake in Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:03.040
without having to tell anyone. Knowing Apple&nbsp;
though, they probably avoid these hot companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.080
If they do have stakes in public companies, it’s&nbsp;
probably in a background company like Qualcomm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.720
While we don’t exactly know these investments&nbsp;
are, as these investments grow, so does Apple’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.440
balance sheet. And at that point, it’s just a&nbsp;
matter of time until that growth is reflected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.320
in their stock price. So, it’s more than worth&nbsp;
it for Apple to use debt to fund investments.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.720
APPLE’S DEBT LOAD:&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.480
In the end, while Apple is more leveraged than&nbsp;
ever before, it’s not exactly a bad thing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.360
it’s extremely strategic. It’s not like Apple&nbsp;
is taking on credit card debt at 20% interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.000
rates to buy a new TV or something. Rather, Apple&nbsp;
is taking on debt at historically low interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.720
rates to better navigate high inflation while also&nbsp;
pumping up their stock price both technically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:52.080
fundamentally. And honestly, this is a brilliant&nbsp;
way to protect the long term future of the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.560
and it’s shareholders. With that being said&nbsp;
though, Apple does have to be careful because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:02.080
there’s fine line between smart debt and stupid&nbsp;
debt. And it’s not that hard to end up with too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:07.760
much debt that ends up weighing down the company.&nbsp;
I mean have you ever heard of 2008? So, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.240
Apple has been playing their cards well so far,&nbsp;
they’ll have to keep a close eye on the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.800
to make sure it doesn’t get out of hand, but&nbsp;
that’s just what I think. Would you be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.760
loan Apple money? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if this video gave you a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.080
perspective on debt. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

